      "id": "fb-001",
      "question": "What happens when a SIM goes offline?",
      "shape": "document",
      "guard": "fix",
      "blocked_on": null,
      "asked_as": "1a1db65e-52d5-493b-a1a9-7e495005530a",
      "http_status": 200,
      "seconds": 48.94,
      "answer": "When a SIM goes offline, it can lead to several challenges and actions depending on the role and responsibilities within the organization:\n\n1. **Fleet Managers**: They need each SIM to be linked to a specific tracker, asset, branch, and department to avoid confusion during support calls and speed up troubleshooting.\n\n2. **Finance Managers**: They require data-spend summaries to monitor connectivity expenses and prevent unnoticed margin erosion.\n\n3. **System Providers**: They aim for the CMS to automatically ingest SIM data from telco APIs to avoid manual updates and improve accuracy.\n\n4. **Technical Installers**: They follow guided SIM-commissioning steps to ensure correct installation and reduce repeat visits.\n\n5. **Cross-Border Fleet Owners**: They need to see roaming status and cost impact when a unit crosses a border to avoid shock bills.\n\n6. **Logistics Managers**: They rely on the system to recommend suitable roaming or local bundles to keep units online at a lower cost.\n\n7. **Support Engineers**: They monitor bundle remaining percentages and projected depletion dates to intervene before a unit goes offline.\n\n8. **Field Staff Supervisors**: They check network type, signal strength, and connectivity gaps to determine if staff are unreachable or inactive.\n\nThese specifications are part of the design and user stories documented in the OLIWA User Stories document. They outline the intended functionalities and considerations for managing SIM cards within the system. \n\nSource: OLIWA User Stories — this is the documented design; I can't confirm from here which parts are live.",
